  rather than at the end of anesthesia, was more effective in preventing PONV.ImplicationsWe evaluated the effect of    
  timing of dexamethasone administration on its efficacy as a prophylactic antiemetic on postoperative nausea and       
  vomiting. We found that dexamethasone, when given immediately before the induction of anesthesia, was more effective  
  than when given at the end of anesthesia.Lippincott Williams & Wilkins'}, {'study_index': 793, 'title': 'Reduction    
  of postoperative vomiting by preoperative administration of oral metoclopramide', 'abstract': 'Preoperative oral      
